Fix int() on Basix 0.8's Python enums when built against nanobind 2.x.

basix.utils.Enum wraps the nanobind enum in a Python Enum, so for a member
such as basix.CellType.triangle, self.value is the nanobind member rather
than an integer. nanobind 2.0 began binding enums as real Python enums,
which have no __int__, so int(self.value) raises TypeError. That breaks
int() on every public enum in cell, finite_element, lattice, maps,
polynomials, quadrature and sobolev_spaces.

This patches installed library code, not just tests. There is no upstream
commit to cite: 0.9 dropped the Python wrapper entirely and exposes the
nanobind enums directly, so the bug exists only on the deprecated 0.8
release. getattr keeps the expression correct for both nanobind generations.

diff --git a/python/basix/utils.py b/python/basix/utils.py
--- a/python/basix/utils.py
+++ b/python/basix/utils.py
@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@ class Enum(_Enum):
 
     def __int__(self) -> int:
         """Convert to int."""
-        return int(self.value)
+        return int(getattr(self.value, "value", self.value))
 
 
 def index(p: int, q: typing.Optional[int] = None, r: typing.Optional[int] = None) -> int:
